<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by the Trinity Choir</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1911</span><span class="sg-chain-h">popular recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Psalm 98</span><span class="sg-chain-h">paraphrased</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Genesis 3:17–19</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alluded to in the third stanza</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Vom Himmel hoch da komm ich her (Martin Luther)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible common source of the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lift up your heads and Comfort ye, from Messiah (George Frideric Handel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1742</span><span class="sg-chain-h">resemblance in the tune, attributed &quot;From Handel&quot; by Mason</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Comfort</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1832</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier tune closely resembling &quot;Antioch&quot;</span></li></ul></section>
